body:
**Shape.** A consume after a dominating explicit kill draws §28.2's warning (correct, v0.345 reads a consume as a use), but the consume's *post-state* — moved-from (K3.3) — is then lost: a later read of the binding is accepted with no diagnostic and reads a null slot.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body()
    var a := new R(2, new Q(1));
    a.Dispose();
    R.Take(move a);                   // §28.2: a consume after a dominating kill is a warning (admitted)
    a.Use();                          // a read of a MOVED-FROM binding: K1.3 error; accepted silently, SIGSEGV in checked mode
  end Body;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(): int
    R.Body();
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** Both modes: one warning (`move from a local \`a\` already disposed at this point (§28.2)`), then `D:2 D:1` and **SIGSEGV** (exit 139) at `a.Use()` — in `--checked-disposal` too, so the deterministic `DisposedAccessException` promise of §28.2 does not hold here. Sibling shapes with the same acceptance: `a.Dispose(); Take(move a); var c := a;` (a borrow of the moved-from binding), `a.Dispose(); Take(move a); Take(move a);` (a second consume), `a.Dispose(); Take(move a); a.f := new Q();`.

A further sibling with the same mechanism but a different rule: `using var b := …; b.Dispose(); var a := b; a.Dispose();` — the alias `a` of the explicitly-disposed `b` may call `Dispose()` (accepted with the §28.2 warning only), although `a` is a borrow and §28.6 makes `Dispose()` through a borrow a compile error with no exception.

**Spec (v0.345).** K3.3 (§28.3.1): an `owned` argument leaves the binding **moved-from**; K1.3: *"moved-from — the obligation has transferred out; the binding is unreadable until reassigned"*; §28.2's warning regime applies to the *explicitly-disposed* state only (K1's auxiliary state: *"a straight-line read falls under §28.2's use-after-dispose warning regime rather than the moved-from error"*) — once the consume has happened the binding is moved-from, and the read is the K1.3 **error**. Expected: the warning at the `move`, then a compile error at `a.Use()`.

**Related.** #3979 / #4122 describe the mechanism (`checkRead`'s §28.2 warning poisons the record before `markMovedByName` runs, so the moved-from state is never recorded); they ask for the `move` itself to be rejected, which v0.345's §28.2 letter no longer supports (*"a consume exactly as a read does"* draws the warning). The defect that remains under v0.345 is the lost post-state and the silently accepted later read.

**Differential numbers.** 31 of 9,000 sampled programs across the pins/temps/fields fragments; every one is a model `K1.3-read-of-moved` rejection that r35 accepts with only the §28.2 warning.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
